  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gerrit Cole gave them enough. Three hits and six strikeouts decided it instead.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole worked into the sixth, allowing four runs on seven hits without breaking. The bullpen cleaned up after him. Headrick struck out three in a scoreless frame, Doval and Blackburn following suit. But the offense never answered Boston's early run in the first, and the game stayed out of reach after the third inning. They had chances in the seventh with runners in scoring position and converted none of them. One run on three hits. That's not an offensive problem. That's an offensive failure.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what slipping looks like. The trend isn't a direction yet. It's a warning. Down 3-7 over the last ten games, they're holding .241 as a team while playing in a division that doesn't forgive inconsistency. Cole was good enough to win. The bullpen was good enough to win. But a team that leaves seven on base and scores once doesn't get to claim it tried hard. The margin between fragile and broken is smaller than it feels.",
